  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Finding cracks in your drywall can be a real bummer, but don't worry, many of them are totally fixable! The first step is to figure out the size and type of damage. For small cracks or holes, like from a nail or screw, you might just need a little joint compound and a putty knife. For slightly larger holes, a self-adhesive mesh patch can work wonders. You just stick it over the hole, then apply thin coats of joint compound, feathering the edges out so it blends seamlessly. If you’re dealing with a bigger opening, say fist-sized or larger, you'll need to cut a piece of drywall to fit, secure it with backing material, and then use joint tape and compound. Remember, it's all about applying thin coats and letting each one dry completely—which can take several hours, sometimes overnight. Sanding lightly between coats helps get that smooth finish. Don't rush it! And if those cracks keep reappearing, or if the damage is extensive, it's a good idea to bring in someone who deals with this stuff all the time, like Abraham and his crew at HH Remodeling LLC. We're here to help make those walls look new again."
    }
  ],
